WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:07.680
My name is Tom Mock.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:10.235
I'm the Workbench product
manager here at Posit.

00:00:10.235 --> 00:00:14.715
I kind of built my
career thinking about the developer experience.

00:00:14.715 --> 00:00:18.075
So worked in the Quarto team
as a product manager thinking

00:00:18.075 --> 00:00:21.680
about how we integrate R
and Python together for notebooks

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:22.720
and extending them,

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:25.680
and now I'm focusing almost
all of my time on the developer

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:26.985
experience for Workbench.

00:00:26.985 --> 00:00:29.945
And integrating R and Python
there for data scientists and

00:00:29.945 --> 00:00:32.802
their daily work.

00:00:32.802 --> 00:00:36.620
Posit Team is kind of our

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:39.500
crown jewel or bundle of all
of our professional products.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:41.900
So you have three kind of
parts to this that all are

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:44.220
complementary, but
also isolated products.

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:46.645
So maybe you only need one
or maybe you need all of them.

00:00:46.645 --> 00:00:49.605
But y'all get to make that
choice in terms of flexibility.

00:00:49.605 --> 00:00:52.010
So Posit Workbench
is your workbench,

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:54.250
where your data scientists,
your data analysts,

00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:58.330
the people who write R, Python,
SQL, they get their work done.

00:00:58.330 --> 00:01:01.370
We provide an authenticated
workspace with, you know,

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:03.755
best-in-class enterprise, single
sign-on, and SAML,

00:01:03.755 --> 00:01:05.515
and the things
you would expect.

00:01:05.515 --> 00:01:08.315
But we also provide
multiple environments for those data

00:01:08.315 --> 00:01:10.950
scientists to get their
work done. So, of course,

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:14.230
you have RStudio for people who are
maybe doing a mix of R and

00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:16.310
Python. You can
execute code there.

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:19.545
We've also got more traditional
and kind of expected pure

00:01:19.545 --> 00:01:20.505
Python environments.

00:01:20.505 --> 00:01:24.265
So we have access to Jupyter
Lab as well as classic Jupyter notebooks.

00:01:24.265 --> 00:01:25.385
And then VS Code,

00:01:25.385 --> 00:01:28.280
which is this very open
ended and flexible integrated

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:31.640
development environment is
also available on Workbench.

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:34.440
Positive Workbench
provides an IDE,

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:36.495
VS Code, Jupyter, Jupyter Labs,

00:01:36.495 --> 00:01:39.455
or RStudio some place to
do that work. Eventually though,

00:01:39.455 --> 00:01:42.095
you wanna get it off
of that environment,

00:01:42.095 --> 00:01:44.630
where you can both track how
it's performing there,

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:47.030
but also how it affects
part of your business,

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:49.750
or integrate with other
parts of your company.

00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:52.070
So as soon as you're ready
to get it off of your local

00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:54.485
environment, you can
deploy it to Connect.

00:01:54.485 --> 00:01:57.845
You can have a command line
where you kind of write it up

00:01:57.845 --> 00:02:01.510
the application and then you
deploy it with authentication into Connect.

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:04.870
But you can also integrate it
into version control or kind of

00:02:04.870 --> 00:02:07.190
continuous integration
and continuous deployment,

00:02:07.190 --> 00:02:10.375
where it goes into something
like GitHub or GitLab and then

00:02:10.375 --> 00:02:12.935
gets pulled automatically
into Connect.

00:02:12.935 --> 00:02:16.295
The last part of it
would be Posit Package Manager. Again,

00:02:16.295 --> 00:02:18.420
with open source, you
have all flexibility,

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:21.620
but with flexibility
can sometimes come complexity.

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:24.420
Package Manager is really this
backbone behind all of our

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:27.265
other tooling. Ultimately, to
do open source data science,

00:02:27.265 --> 00:02:30.625
you need open source data
science libraries and packages.

00:02:30.625 --> 00:02:33.585
So Posit Package Manager allows
you to host both your own

00:02:33.585 --> 00:02:35.970
Python packages, so you
can create your own wheels,

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:38.530
upload them to Posit Package
Manager and have them stored

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:43.095
there, as well as grab subsets
or all of PyPi, and kind of

00:02:43.095 --> 00:02:46.535
the other open source packages that
the whole community is working on.

00:02:46.535 --> 00:02:50.135
So that really takes away a
lot of the headache of having

00:02:50.135 --> 00:02:52.700
to manage more complex
structures like you don't have

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:55.740
to use Docker, you don't
have to use tools like Conda.

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:58.225
You don't have to manage all
these system dependencies

00:02:58.225 --> 00:03:01.425
you're really focused on
purely writing Python code and

00:03:01.425 --> 00:03:04.945
indicating what are the
libraries or packages that

00:03:04.945 --> 00:03:06.950
provide the
functionality I want.

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:12.150
So Python at its most simple
is part of what Posit Team provides for you.

00:03:14.945 --> 00:03:17.985
Posit historically has been
known for the RStudio IDE,

00:03:17.985 --> 00:03:22.545
that's kind of our crown jewel
of developer experience for R users.

00:03:22.545 --> 00:03:25.340
We've done a lot of investments
for Python users as well,

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:28.460
however. And so we have
Quarto, we have Vetiver,

00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:31.900
we have all these open source
tools that really try to make

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:34.445
data science seamless
for Python users.

00:03:34.445 --> 00:03:37.725
So part of our investment in
open source is that we are both

00:03:37.725 --> 00:03:39.885
doing it ourselves
and integrating with others.

00:03:39.885 --> 00:03:41.900
So if you think about
Posit the company,

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:43.820
about half of our
company's engineering,

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:47.820
and about half of our engineers
are working purely on open source.

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:50.860
So we're making huge
investments in terms of people

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:54.285
hours in improving,
and maintaining

00:03:54.285 --> 00:03:58.525
professionally supported open source
data science packages. Over time,

00:03:58.525 --> 00:04:01.320
you might have shifts in
how you're developing your your

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:03.240
tooling or writing your models,

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:06.520
and we want to kind of
future proof the platform so that as

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:07.400
new frameworks come out,

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:09.400
we're adding those in
and making sure that they're

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:11.375
supported and we're
testing against them,

00:04:11.375 --> 00:04:14.515
as well as allowing them to
be deployed into things on production.

